## Proof

Let $A = 8^{9^{8^{9}}}$ and $B = 9^{8^{9^{8}}}$, with the standard right-associative convention for exponentiation:
$$A = 8^{\left(9^{\left(8^{9}\right)}\right)}, \qquad B = 9^{\left(8^{\left(9^{8}\right)}\right)}.$$

Taking natural logarithms twice (all quantities are positive, so this is legitimate):

$$\ln\ln A = 8^{9}\,\ln 9 + \ln\ln 8, \qquad \ln\ln B = 9^{8}\,\ln 8 + \ln\ln 9.$$

So
$$\ln\ln A - \ln\ln B = \underbrace{\bigl(8^{9}\ln 9 - 9^{8}\ln 8\bigr)}_{(\ast)} + \underbrace{\bigl(\ln\ln 8 - \ln\ln 9\bigr)}_{(\ast\ast)}.$$

**Term $(\ast)$ is positive and large.** Compute directly:
$$8^{9} = 134\,217\,728, \qquad 9^{8} = 43\,046\,721,$$
so $8^{9} > 9^{8}$. Since also $\ln 9 > \ln 8 > 0$, both factors satisfy $8^{9} > 9^{8}$ and $\ln 9 > \ln 8$, giving
$$8^{9}\ln 9 > 9^{8}\ln 8.$$
In fact $8^{9}\ln 9 - 9^{8}\ln 8 \approx 2.05\times 10^{8}$.

**Term $(\ast\ast)$ is bounded and negligible.** Since $8 < 9 < e^{e}$, we have $\ln\ln 8, \ln\ln 9 \in (0,1)$, so
$$|\ln\ln 8 - \ln\ln 9| < 1.$$

**Combining.** The positive term $(\ast)\approx 2\times 10^{8}$ overwhelms the bounded term $(\ast\ast)$ of absolute value $<1$:
$$\ln\ln A - \ln\ln B > 0.$$

Since $\ln\ln$ is strictly increasing on $(1,\infty)$ and both $A,B > e$, it follows that
$$\ln A > \ln B \implies A > B.$$

$$\boxed{8^{9^{8^{9}}} \;>\; 9^{8^{9^{8}}}.}$$

